<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  CycloneDDS configuration for the x86 HOST side.

  Mounted read-only at /cfg/cyclonedds.xml and selected with
  CYCLONEDDS_URI=file:///cfg/cyclonedds.xml.

  ESTE E O TEMPLATE. O compose monta `host.rendered.xml`, que
  `scripts/module.sh sync` gera a partir daqui somando o endereco do modulo e a
  interface deste host. O gerado nao e versionado porque endereco nao entra em
  git (convencoes do CLAUDE.md).

  CONSEQUENCIA QUE CUSTOU UMA SESSAO, 25/08/2026: editar este arquivo NAO surte
  efeito nenhum nos containers ate rodar `module.sh sync`. O rendered daquela
  bancada tinha sido gerado antes, era byte-identico a este, e ficou parado
  enquanto este mudava. Depois de qualquer edicao aqui:

      scripts/module.sh sync     # regenera host.rendered.xml
      docker compose -f compose.host.yml up -d  (com force-recreate)

  Ha um teste estrutural em tests/ que reprova um rendered mais antigo que este
  arquivo, justamente para essa divergencia nao ficar silenciosa.

  scripts/env.sh aponta para o rendered quando ele existe, e para este arquivo
  quando nao, para que ferramenta nativa no host e container falem a MESMA
  politica de descoberta. Duas politicas no mesmo
  dominio funcionam enquanto a interface escolhida por acidente coincide, e param
  sem avisar quando deixa de coincidir.

  NOTE ON INTERFACE SELECTION
  ===========================
  The ML3.5 guide (§5) shows <NetworkInterface name="eth0"/>. That is a
  PLACEHOLDER and it is wrong on this workstation, which has no eth0 at all:
  `ip -br link` here reports lo, enp0s31f6 (DOWN, no carrier), wlp0s20f3 (UP,
  Wi-Fi), tailscale0, docker0 and bridges.

  Hardcoding a name that does not exist makes CycloneDDS fail to find the
  interface and fall back in a way that looks like a DDS problem rather than a
  config typo. So this file uses autodetermine instead of a name.

  autodetermine picks the highest-priority interface CycloneDDS can find. That is
  usually right on a single-NIC machine and NOT reliably right here, where
  docker0 and tailscale0 are also up and can be chosen ahead of the intended
  link. If host<->module discovery misbehaves, replace the autodetermine line
  with the real interface name, verified first:

      ip -br link                      # on this host
      # then, in this file:
      # <NetworkInterface name="wlp0s20f3" priority="default"/>

  Multicast is off and peers are explicit because multicast commonly dies on
  Wi-Fi and on managed switches. This host is on Wi-Fi (wlp0s20f3), so that is
  the expected case, not a corner case.
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
  <Domain id="any">
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <!--
          LOOPBACK PRIMEIRO, E ELE E A CORRECAO DE 25/08/2026.

          Toda a pilha do modo learn roda com network_mode: host na MESMA
          maquina, entao o caminho correto entre containers e `lo`. Antes desta
          linha o trafego dependia do que o autodetermine abaixo escolhesse, e o
          proprio aviso no cabecalho deste arquivo previa o problema: com
          docker0, duas bridges e tailscale0 no ar, a escolha pode cair numa
          interface que nao roteia para os outros participantes.

          O QUE ISSO CAUSOU, MEDIDO
          Entrega ASSIMETRICA entre containers. O `nav` recebia /demo/odom e
          /demo/scan_cloud do `sim` (os locators do sim eram alcancaveis) e o
          `sim` NAO recebia /demo/cmd_vel do `nav`. O resultado nao se parece com
          um problema de rede: o Nav2 planeja, comanda guinada em 95,6% das
          amostras, e o robo trota PARADO, isto e, `sticks=(lx=0.0000 ly=0.0000
          rx=0.0000)` no log da marcha durante 180 s, com o yaw congelado. Nav2
          entao acusa "Failed to make progress" e entra em recuperacao, que
          tambem nao chega. Zero linhas mencionando DDS ou interface.

          E o log enche de `ddsi_udp_conn_write ... failed with retcode -1` para
          um endereco do proprio host, mas isso e milhoes de linhas de nivel
          baixo no meio da saida do Gazebo, nao um erro que alguem le.

          `lo` existe em qualquer maquina, entao isto NAO e o nome cravado que o
          cabecalho acima condena, isto e, e o unico nome de interface que e seguro
          cravar. Multicast ligado apenas aqui: em loopback ele nao atravessa
          switch nenhum, entao a objecao de "morre no Wi-Fi" nao se aplica.

          A interface real continua na lista, abaixo, para o modo hil alcancar o
          modulo. NAO REMOVA NENHUMA DAS DUAS: sem `lo` volta a assimetria acima;
          sem a real o host nao fala com o Aquila.

          AS PRIORIDADES DESTA COPIA SAO PARA LEARN. `module.sh sync` gera a
          variante HIL e inverte as prioridades: Ethernet=10, lo=default. Foi
          medido em 28/08/2026 que manter lo=10 em HIL faz o Cyclone tentar
          unicast externo pela loopback (`ddsi_udp_conn_write ... retcode -3`):
          /clock aparece no grafo remoto, mas nenhuma amostra atravessa.
        -->
        <NetworkInterface name="lo" priority="10" multicast="true"/>
        <NetworkInterface autodetermine="true" priority="default"/>
      </Interfaces>
      <!--
        Off deliberately. With multicast enabled and peers listed, discovery can
        succeed over multicast on the bench and then fail on the customer's
        network, which hides the misconfiguration until the demo is in front of
        someone.
      -->
      <AllowMulticast>false</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <Peers>
        <!--
          127.0.0.1 IS LOAD-BEARING. DO NOT REMOVE IT.

          With AllowMulticast=false, CycloneDDS discovers ONLY what is listed
          here — and that includes participants in the same container and in
          sibling containers on the same machine. Without a localhost peer there
          is no same-machine discovery path at all.

          Measured, learn mode, all four containers up: without this line
          `ros2 node list` returned nothing, `ros2 topic list` showed only
          /rosout and /parameter_events, and the Gazebo spawner sat forever on
          "Waiting messages on topic [robot_description]" while
          robot_state_publisher was demonstrably running and had logged "Robot
          initialized" in the same container. Nothing named DDS as the cause.

          Note that this is NOT the loopback interface. CycloneDDS selects the
          highest-quality interface (here wlp0s20f3) and transmits on it; the
          localhost peer only adds a discovery address. Forcing
          <NetworkInterface name="lo"/> instead was tested and is WRONG — it
          isolates the client from nodes that already selected the real
          interface.

          THE MODULE IP IS NOT LISTED HERE.
          The ${MODULE_IP} placeholder that used to sit in this file never
          expanded: Docker does not perform variable substitution inside a
          bind-mounted file, so CycloneDDS received the literal string
          "${MODULE_IP}" as an address. The ML3.5 guide §5 shows it that way and
          it cannot work as written.

          For hil mode (F5), add the module's real address as another <Peer>,
          either by editing this file on the host or by generating it from .env
          at compose time. That is F5 work; F1 is host-only and needs only
          localhost.
        -->
        <Peer address="127.0.0.1"/>
      </Peers>
    </Discovery>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
